WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0028: a cake split in a geometric sequence,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.833
Two parts: plugging in numbers,
then the general case with a proof.

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:14.367
As in question 0027, but now the fraction
grows by a factor q, not by a fixed step.

4
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:19.367
A cake of size 1. Each person in turn cuts
a fraction p_k of what is left, not of the whole cake.

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:25.533
An example with a = 0.1 and q = 2: the fractions
0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, and the fifth takes the rest.

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:29.533
The fraction grows, but what is left
to cut from shrinks. Who gets the largest piece?

7
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:35.533
Part A: a = 0.01 and q = 1.5. The fractions
0.01, 0.015, 0.0225, each 1.5 times the last.

8
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:40.967
The cake on top, a chart below:
the share each person gets, a bar per person.

9
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:47.300
The first five cut thin slices:
the fraction is small and the cake is almost whole.

10
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:53.100
The fraction keeps growing by 1.5 and the bars
rise. Up to the ninth, each one gets more.

11
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.100
The tenth takes 0.3844 of what is left
and gets 0.1697: the tallest bar.

12
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.900
After him the fraction is large, but little is left.
The bars fall, and the 13th takes the rest.

13
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.900
The first nine took less than 60% of the cake
together, and from there the fraction jumps fast.

14
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.900
TODO

15
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:19.100
Notation: R_k is what is left before person k.
He takes p_k of it, and the rest passes on.

16
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.600
Compare two neighbours: what is left cancels,
and with p_{k+1} = q·p_k the fraction cancels too.

17
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.767
A very simple ratio is left: q times 1 − p_k.
It does not depend on what is left at all.

18
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:35.067
The next person gets more exactly when the ratio
is above 1, that is, when the fraction is below a third.

19
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:40.467
Here each bar is one person's fraction,
and the dashed line is the threshold, one third.

20
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:45.200
Nine fractions are below the threshold: each of them
is followed by someone who gets more.

21
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.000
The tenth fraction is the first above the threshold.
All the fractions after it are above it.

22
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.600
Who passes the threshold? Multiply by q over a,
since 0.5 over 0.01 is 50.

23
00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.000
Take the logarithm in base 1.5 and round
up: the tenth person.

24
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.500
TODO

25
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.567
A direct check: the ninth fraction is below
the threshold, and the tenth is already above it.

26
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:15.533
And his share: 0.3844 of the 0.4415 left,
that is, about 17% of the cake.

27
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:21.133
This is the page's box: the cake,
and so far nine people in the table.

28
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:25.600
The next person: the tenth cuts 0.3844
of what is left and gets 0.1697.

29
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.833
And the eleventh gets less, 0.1567.
The peak is the tenth person.

30
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:34.567
TODO

31
00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:40.567
Part B: any a and q, with 1 < q ≤ 2.
Who gets the most, and why is there one peak?

32
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:46.467
The neighbours' ratio, q(1 − p_k), does not depend on
Part A's numbers. Here it is as a dot per k.

33
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:51.867
The fractions grow, so the ratio only falls.
Nine dots above the line: the shares grow.

34
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:56.967
The tenth person's dot is the first one
below the line, and all after it stay below.

35
00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:00.967
So there is one peak: the shares grow up to
the first person who drops to the line, then only fall.

36
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:07.167
The threshold is where the ratio equals 1:
T = 1 − 1/q. One third for q = 1.5.

37
00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:11.967
Who passes the threshold: multiply by q over a
and take the logarithm in base q.

38
00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:17.433
And the formula: the first person whose fraction
reaches the threshold. If a is above it, the first.

39
00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:23.733
TODO

40
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:29.233
TODO

41
00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:33.233
TODO

42
00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:39.433
With a = 0.01 and other q: q = 2 gives
the seventh person, with 0.313 of the cake.

43
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:44.933
The closer q is to 1, the later the peak,
low and flat: with 1.1, the 25th person.

44
00:03:45.733 --> 00:03:51.133
When is there a tie? When q^k equals
(q − 1)/a exactly. For example a = 0.25 and q = 2.

45
00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:57.033
The second fraction is exactly a half, the threshold.
The second and third get 0.375 each.

46
00:03:57.633 --> 00:04:03.133
TODO

47
00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:07.133
TODO

48
00:04:07.933 --> 00:04:13.433
In the page's box, at the proof step, each person
is a dot on the line q(1 − p), against y = 1.

49
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:19.433
And with a = 0.25 and q = 2, the second dot
touches the line exactly: a tie with the third.

50
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:23.433
When q is above 2 the last person can break
the formula. The page's follow-ups show when.

51
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:29.833
To sum up: the neighbours' ratio is q(1 − p_k),
and the peak is at the first to pass 1 − 1/q.

52
00:04:29.833 --> 00:04:33.800
Try your own a and q in the box
on the page of question 0028 on the site.
